!!id:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/fixtures/validate-family.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/40/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/40/m/core.tn"
@doc:"""
  A subtype family, both ways a position typed by one is read ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.2).

  `pet` dispatches on its members: it declares a selector (`=?`) and each member pins it, so a value at a `pet` position is
  placed by reading the member it already carries and needs no tag at all. `shape` is ABSTRACT: it declares
  no discriminator, so the tag is the only selector and is required. Both bases are abstract — neither has
  direct instances — and that is what makes a tag naming either of them select nothing.

  `outcome` is a **template** marked ABSTRACT, and `abstract` on a template is each application's `extension`
  ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.10), so `outcome<text>` is an abstract record, declared as `outcome_of`. `won` composes
  `outcome<T>`, so `won<text>`, declared as `won_of`, IS-A the entry `outcome<text>` denotes and is its one
  member; neither is IS-A a template, a template being no type. A document names each by its declaration:
  this is the family where §7.2's "after alias flattening of both" is the difference between a tag that
  selects and one that does not. `final` is refused on a template, and no vector states that here — it is a
  schema-layer refusal.

  `frame` pins with `= 0xFF` where the documents write `255`, which is the case a processor comparing
  tokens rather than values gets wrong while passing everything else here: §4.3 makes those one integer,
  and the pin and the member's value are decoded by the same parser before either is compared.

  `holder` puts each family at a field position. A document's own root type-ref names the position's type,
  so reading a family at the root would be testing the root convention rather than the family.
  """
!schema {
  pet => abstract {
    pet_type: text =?
    name: text
  }

  dog => pet & {
    pet_type?: = "dog"
    breed: text
  }

  cat => pet & {
    pet_type?: = "cat"
    indoor: boolean
  }

  shape => abstract {
    area: int32
  }

  square => shape & {
    side: int32
  }

  frame => abstract {
    opcode: int32 =?
    payload: text
  }

  ping => frame & {
    opcode?: = 0xFF
    seq: int32
  }

  outcome => abstract <T> {
    code: T
  }

  won => <T> outcome<T> & {
    prize: text
  }

  outcome_of => outcome<text>
  won_of     => won<text>

  holder => {
    p: pet
    s?: shape?
    f?: frame?
    o?: outcome_of?
  }
}
